/>
```

The component needs a parent with a defined available width so React Native can lay out and measure the text. The button is kept visible at the end of the line; the text may be ellipsized to make room for it.

## Evaluation

Assistant B is closer to a usable answer: it gives a relevant component, a usage example, and uses React Native’s text-layout APIs. However, its key overflow check is flawed. The text being measured has `numberOfLines={1}`, so `onTextLayout` will generally report only the limited, displayed line—not the full text’s line count. As a result, `showSeeMore` may never become true. And even if it does, placing “See more” inside that same one-line, line-limited `<Text>` can cause the label itself to be truncated. B also overstates the code as production-ready.

Assistant A’s response has much more serious problems: it contains invalid or malformed JSX and JavaScript, undefined variables and components, conflicting uses of `text`, and logic that does not implement the requested behavior. Its feature claims, including the SEO-related claims, are unsupported by the code. It is not a functioning component.

Neither answer fully solves the task. B is nevertheless more relevant and coherent than A, though its central implementation bug is significant. My answer addresses that issue by measuring the full text separately and reserving space for a visible button.

My final verdict is: [[B>>A]]

